[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":208},"en/routes/conteudo-do-cubo.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10,"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":20,"_21":-7,"_22":23,"_24":25},"tipo","metodo","cubo",{"_11":206,"_13":206,"_27":207},"id","roux","nome","Roux","nomeLongo","Roux (blocks, CMLL and the last 6 edges)","termo",{"_27":203,"_204":205},"niveis",[50],"introducao","etapas",[31,32,33,34],"historias",[26],{"_13":14,"_27":28,"_29":30},"endereco","/historia#roux","resumo","Created by Frenchman Gilles Roux, who got back into cubing in 2001 and wanted an almost fully intuitive method, finished with just M and U.",{"_11":196,"_13":197,"_37":198,"_39":199,"_41":200,"_43":-7},{"_11":118,"_13":119,"_37":120,"_39":121,"_41":122,"_43":123},{"_11":67,"_13":68,"_37":69,"_39":70,"_41":-7,"_43":71},{"_11":35,"_13":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":42,"_43":44},"lse","LSE (last 6 edges)","html","<p>With just M and U: orient the 6 edges (4a), put UL and UR in place (4b) and finish the 4 of the M slice (4c). Almost everything is intuitive; <a href=\"/en/glossary#eo\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Edge orientation: each edge turned so that it can go to its place without using F and B.\">EO</a> 4a has the cases with algorithms.</p>\n","grupos",[47],"pagina",{"_13":45,"_27":46},"treino","/3x3/treino?grupo=eo-4a","LSE: the last 6 edges (Roux)","/3x3/lse",{"_11":48,"_13":48,"_49":50,"_51":52,"_53":54,"_55":56,"_27":57},"EO 4a","nivel","intermediario","casos",9,"conjunto",false,"ids",[58,59,60,61,62,63,64,65,66],"/3x3/eo-4a","EO4a-All6","EO4a-2Top2Bot","EO4a-Bottom2","EO4a-Top2Front2","EO4a-4Top","EO4a-2TopOpp","EO4a-2TopAdj","EO4a-1Top1Bot","EO4a-2TopAdj2Bot","cmll","CMLL","<p>The top corners at once, without touching the <a href=\"/en/glossary#bloco\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A set of pieces solved together; in Roux, the 1x2x3 blocks on the sides.\">blocks</a>. You can start in two steps (orient with <a href=\"/en/glossary#oll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Makes the top face all one color, without caring about the position of the pieces. There are 57 cases; in 2-Look OLL, 10 cases in two parts.\">OLL</a> algorithms and then permute) and swap them for the 42 <a href=\"/en/glossary#cmll\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"In Roux: orients and permutes the 4 top corners, without caring about the top edges or the middle layer.\">CMLL</a> cases over time.</p>\n",[72],"/3x3/treino?grupo=cmll",{"_11":68,"_13":68,"_49":50,"_51":73,"_53":54,"_55":74,"_27":75},42,[76,77,78,79,80,81,82,83,84,85,86,87,88,89,90,91,92,93,94,95,96,97,98,99,100,101,102,103,104,105,106,107,108,109,110,111,112,113,114,115,116,117],"/3x3/cmll","CMLL-OAdjacent","CMLL-ODiagonal","CMLL-HColumns","CMLL-HRows","CMLL-HColumn","CMLL-HRow","CMLL-PiRightBar","CMLL-PiDownSlash","CMLL-PiX","CMLL-PiUpSlash","CMLL-PiColumns","CMLL-PiLeftBar","CMLL-UUpSlash","CMLL-UDownSlash","CMLL-UBottomRow","CMLL-URows","CMLL-UX","CMLL-UUpperRow","CMLL-TLeftBar","CMLL-TRightBar","CMLL-TRows","CMLL-TBottomRow","CMLL-TTopRow","CMLL-TColumns","CMLL-SuneLeftBar","CMLL-SuneX","CMLL-SuneUpSlash","CMLL-SuneColumns","CMLL-SuneRightBar","CMLL-SuneDownSlash","CMLL-AntiSuneRightBar","CMLL-AntiSuneColumns","CMLL-AntiSuneDownSlash","CMLL-AntiSuneX","CMLL-AntiSuneUpSlash","CMLL-AntiSuneLeftBar","CMLL-LBest","CMLL-LGood","CMLL-LPure","CMLL-LFrontCommutator","CMLL-LDiagonal","CMLL-LBackCommutator","segundo-bloco","2nd block","<p>The mirror of the 1st, on the right side, without touching it: only <code>R</code>, <code>r</code>, <code>U</code> and <code>M</code>. First the bottom edge, then the back square and, finally, the front <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pair</a>, which has the <a href=\"/en/glossary#sbls\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"In Roux: finishes the second block (the right one) by inserting the last pair.\">SBLS</a> cases.</p>\n",[126],{"_13":124,"_27":125},"/3x3/treino?grupo=sbls","2nd block (Roux)","/3x3/segundo-bloco",{"_11":127,"_13":127,"_49":50,"_51":128,"_53":54,"_55":129,"_27":130},"SBLS",65,[131,132,133,134,135,136,137,138,139,140,141,142,143,144,145,146,147,148,149,150,151,152,153,154,155,156,157,158,159,160,161,162,163,164,165,166,167,168,169,170,171,172,173,174,175,176,177,178,179,180,181,182,183,184,185,186,187,188,189,190,191,192,193,194,195],"/3x3/sbls","SBLS-UUB","SBLS-UUR","SBLS-UUF","SBLS-UUL","SBLS-UFR","SBLS-UDB","SBLS-UDF","SBLS-UUBm","SBLS-UURm","SBLS-UUFm","SBLS-UULm","SBLS-UFRm","SBLS-UDBm","SBLS-UDFm","SBLS-UpUB","SBLS-UpUR","SBLS-UpUF","SBLS-UpUL","SBLS-UpFR","SBLS-UpDB","SBLS-UpDF","SBLS-UpUBm","SBLS-UpURm","SBLS-UpUFm","SBLS-UpULm","SBLS-UpFRm","SBLS-UpDBm","SBLS-UpDFm","SBLS-UmUB","SBLS-UmUR","SBLS-UmUF","SBLS-UmUL","SBLS-UmFR","SBLS-UmDB","SBLS-UmDF","SBLS-UmUBm","SBLS-UmURm","SBLS-UmUFm","SBLS-UmULm","SBLS-UmFRm","SBLS-UmDBm","SBLS-UmDFm","SBLS-DU","SBLS-DDB","SBLS-DDF","SBLS-DUm","SBLS-DFRm","SBLS-DDBm","SBLS-DDFm","SBLS-DpU","SBLS-DpFR","SBLS-DpDB","SBLS-DpDF","SBLS-DpUm","SBLS-DpFRm","SBLS-DpDBm","SBLS-DpDFm","SBLS-DmU","SBLS-DmFR","SBLS-DmDB","SBLS-DmDF","SBLS-DmUm","SBLS-DmFRm","SBLS-DmDBm","SBLS-DmDFm","primeiro-bloco","1st block","<p>In <a href=\"/en/glossary#roux\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Gilles Roux's method: two 1x2x3 blocks on the sides, CMLL and the last 6 edges (LSE) with the middle layer. It uses few moves.\">Roux</a>, build a 1×2×3 <a href=\"/en/glossary#bloco\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A set of pieces solved together; in Roux, the 1x2x3 blocks on the sides.\">block</a> on the left and another on the right, leaving the middle slice free. It&#39;s all intuitive, like <a href=\"/en/glossary#cfop\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The most used method in competitions: the cross, the 4 F2L pairs, OLL (makes the top face one color) and PLL (puts the top pieces in place).\">CFOP</a>&#39;s intuitive <a href=\"/en/glossary#f2l\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The first two layers. In CFOP, each bottom corner goes together with the middle edge above it (a pair), one pair at a time.\">F2L</a>; the last <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pair</a> of the 2nd block has algorithms (<a href=\"/en/glossary#sbls\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"In Roux: finishes the second block (the right one) by inserting the last pair.\">SBLS</a>) for the annoying cases.</p>\n",[],{"_13":201,"_27":202},"1st block (Roux)","/3x3/primeiro-bloco","/en/glossary#roux","dica","Gilles Roux's method: two 1x2x3 blocks on the sides, CMLL and the last 6 edges (LSE) with the middle layer. It uses few moves.","3x3","/3x3/",{"_209":210,"_211":212},"cubos",[215,216,217,218,219,220],"idiomas",[213,214],"pt","en",{"_11":206,"_13":206},{"_11":227,"_13":227},{"_11":226,"_13":226},{"_11":225,"_13":225},{"_11":223,"_13":224},{"_11":221,"_13":222},"pyraminx","Pyraminx","megaminx","Megaminx","5x5","4x4","2x2"]
